## Decoding the question
A **10-mark note** (about **550–650 words**). Two concepts must be joined: **world-system theory** (Wallerstein) and the **international sexual division of labour** — how work is divided by gender **across** the world economy, not only within households. Explain the theory, then the feminist extension, then Pakistan.

## The model answer

### 1. Wallerstein's world-system

#### i) One world-economy
**Immanuel Wallerstein** (*The Modern World-System*, ==1974==) argued that since the sixteenth century there has been a single capitalist **world-economy**. It differs from dependency theory by treating the world as **one system**, adding a middle zone — the semi-periphery — and tracing a history of five centuries.

#### ii) Three zones

##### (a) The core
Rich countries specialising in finance, technology and high-value production.

##### (b) The semi-periphery
Countries in between, which both exploit the periphery and are exploited by the core.

##### (c) The periphery
Countries supplying raw materials and cheap labour; surplus flows to the core through **unequal exchange**.

::: fig pyramid | The world-system — and where women's labour sits within it.
Core: finance, technology, brands; consumers of cheap goods and care
Semi-periphery: assembly and export manufacturing
Periphery: raw materials, cheap labour; women at the bottom of global chains
:::

### 2. The sexual division of labour

#### i) The idea
The **sexual division of labour** assigns different work to women and men — men to paid, "productive" work and women to unpaid, "reproductive" care.

#### ii) Its international form
The **international sexual division of labour** describes how global capitalism draws on this division across countries: women in the periphery supply the cheapest labour for global production and care.

### 3. Feminist extensions of world-system theory

#### i) The household
**Joan Smith and Immanuel Wallerstein** (==1992==) showed that households combine wages with **unpaid work**, so employers can pay **below a living wage** — women's unpaid labour fills the gap.

#### ii) Mies
**Maria Mies** (*Patriarchy and Accumulation on a World Scale*, ==1986==) linked the exploitation of **nature, colonies and women**, and described "**housewifisation**" — defining women as housewives so their paid work could be treated as a supplement and paid less. Her study of the **lace makers of Narsapur** in India showed women producing for world markets at home while being counted as housewives doing leisure work.

#### iii) The global assembly line
**Annette Fuentes and Barbara Ehrenreich** (==1983==) described the "**global assembly line**" of young women in export factories.

### 4. Care chains and the feminisation of labour

#### i) Feminisation of labour
**Guy Standing** (==1989==) showed that global competition made jobs more **flexible, informal and low-paid** — "feminised" — and drew women into them.

#### ii) Global care chains
**Arlie Hochschild** (==2000==) described poor women migrating to care for richer families' children, leaving their own children with relatives — a transfer of care from periphery to core.

#### iii) The evidence of harm
The **Rana Plaza** collapse in Bangladesh (==24 April 2013==) killed more than 1,100 people, mostly women garment workers.

### 5. Pakistan's place in the system

#### i) Periphery or lower semi-periphery
Pakistan exports textiles, cotton and rice and depends on external finance.

#### ii) Women at the bottom
Women work at the bottom of global chains — cotton picking, home-based stitching and embroidery for piece rates — while men migrate to the Gulf; remittances reached a record of more than **38 billion dollars** in ==2024–25==. Home-based workers — Mies's "housewifisation" in action — won legal recognition first in **Sindh**, through the **Home-Based Workers Act 2018**.

#### iii) Leverage
The EU's **GSP+** status (==2014==), tied to 27 conventions including **CEDAW**, gives some leverage for labour and women's rights.

### 6. Evaluation

#### i) Insight
The theory explains why women's labour is cheap across the world economy and links household inequality to global capitalism.

#### ii) Limits
It risks portraying women as **passive victims** and underplays local patriarchy and women's agency; **Naila Kabeer** (*The Power to Choose*, ==2000==) found that factory work gave many Bangladeshi women their first independent income and greater bargaining power.

#### iii) The line to take
Global capitalism both **exploits** and **opens** opportunities for women; outcomes depend on labour rights, state policy and women's organising.

## Why this answer scores
- Explains **Wallerstein's three zones** precisely and joins them to the **sexual division of labour**.
- Names the feminist extensions with dates (Mies 1986, Smith and Wallerstein 1992, Fuentes and Ehrenreich 1983, Standing 1989, Hochschild 2000).
- Applies the theory to **Pakistan** (textiles, home-based work, remittances, GSP+).
- Evaluates with **Kabeer's** counter-evidence; a **pyramid** figure maps women's place in the system.
